    "guide": "Machine learning operations and infrastructure work centers on keeping models and the pipelines that produce them running in production, not on inventing the models themselves. Engineers in this discipline build and maintain the systems that train, serve, monitor, and scale machine learning workloads, often across large GPU clusters. A typical week might involve provisioning Kubernetes clusters, tuning distributed training jobs, debugging why a model server is dropping requests under load, or building the CI/CD pipeline that pushes a new model version safely into production. This is different from machine learning engineering, which focuses more on building and improving the models and their architectures, and from data engineering, which focuses on the pipelines that move and clean data before it reaches a model. In our postings, employers often blend infrastructure, systems, and platform language into a single job description, so titles like ML Platform Engineer, ML Systems Engineer, and AI Infrastructure Engineer frequently describe very similar underlying work.\n\nDegrees matter less here than in research-heavy machine learning roles. None of the postings we reviewed named a required degree or a specific cloud certification such as an AWS or Azure credential, even though AWS, Azure, and GCP appear constantly as required platform experience. What employers ask for instead is direct, hands-on experience running production infrastructure, often for large language models or GPU-heavy training workloads. A computer science or engineering degree is common among candidates in this field, but it functions as a baseline rather than a differentiator. Where cloud certifications exist in the broader industry, our data suggests employers here treat them as decorative rather than as a stated requirement.\n\nEntry into this field usually comes through a software engineering or systems background rather than a pure data science path. Junior and mid-level roles, which make up the largest share of what we track, ask for solid programming skills plus exposure to containers, cloud infrastructure, and at least one deep learning framework. Senior roles add expectations around distributed systems design, incident response, and owning a platform end to end rather than a single service. At the staff, principal, and lead level, postings shift toward architecture ownership, cross-team technical leadership, and in several cases direct responsibility for capacity planning or GPU fleet operations at scale. A separate cluster of postings sits at the manager level, covering technical program management and product management roles focused specifically on AI infrastructure and supply chain rather than hands-on engineering.\n\nThe skills listed across these postings point to a consistent toolkit. Kubernetes, Docker, and Terraform show up repeatedly as the baseline for deploying and managing infrastructure, alongside Python as the dominant language and Rust and C++ appearing in performance-sensitive roles. PyTorch, TensorFlow, and JAX are the most common machine learning frameworks named, with CUDA, Triton, and general GPU infrastructure experience required wherever training or serving happens on GPU clusters. AWS, Azure, and GCP all appear as named cloud platforms, and CI/CD pipeline experience is called out directly in a number of listings. The term MLOps itself appears as an explicit skill tag in several postings, alongside distributed systems and incident response experience, which points to reliability and automation being treated as core competencies rather than nice-to-haves.\n\nBased on today's snapshot of our database, we currently track approximately 58 open postings in this category, out of 85 distinct postings we have observed recently overall. Only 11 of those postings listed an actual salary figure, so any range here should be read as a thin sample rather than a market average. Among those 11, stated salaries ran from about 91,000 dollars to about 277,000 dollars a year, with most of them falling somewhere in the 120,000 to 200,000 dollar range. Scale AI, Cerebras, OpenAI, Together AI, Nebius, and Anthropic appear most often as hiring companies in our data, reflecting how concentrated current hiring activity is among frontier AI labs and infrastructure-focused startups. Readers should treat company frequency as a signal of who is actively posting right now, not as a ranking of who offers the strongest roles.",
